WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.834
-Our next guest
is a talented poet and author

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:04.868
whose debut novel,
"Nightcrawling," is out now.

00:00:04.868 --> 00:00:07.901
Please welcome to the show
Leila Mottley, everybody.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:09.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:19.400
♪♪

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.968
♪♪

00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:24.067
-Welcome to the show, Leila.

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:26.033
-Thanks for having me.
-This is very exciting.

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:27.667
Your book
is not technically out now,

00:00:27.667 --> 00:00:30.133
but by the time this show airs,
it will be.

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:31.868
Obviously,
you've put years into it.

00:00:31.868 --> 00:00:33.234
How does it feel
knowing the world

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:35.033
will soon be able to read it?

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:39.133
-I still can't fathom that it's
not just living in my head.

00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:41.033
So when people come up to me
and they're like,

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:43.734
"I read your book,"
I'm like, "You did?"

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:45.901
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:49.100
So it's surreal, for sure.
-So now, this is the time

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.834
where I say something
that I'm very impressed by,

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:52.901
but I think we both know
it's gonna make some people

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:54.567
hate you a little bit.
-Yeah.

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.200
-Tell everybody how old you are.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.067
-I turn 20 next week.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:00.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.267
-Oh.
So, Leila, you are --

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.834
You are currently
a college student.

00:01:06.834 --> 00:01:08.367
-Yes, I'm on break.
-Yeah.

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.534
You're studying writing?
-English, yep.

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:12.934
-Yeah. So how do you think
your professors are going

00:01:12.934 --> 00:01:14.767
to, um, uh...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:17.467
...deal with you moving forward?

00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:21.868
-Um, I hope that they
do it exactly the same.

00:01:21.868 --> 00:01:25.434
I want the same feedback.
But you never know.

00:01:25.434 --> 00:01:27.834
I don't know.
-Now, obviously, you've --

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:30.534
I'm guessing you've had great
teachers over the years,

00:01:30.534 --> 00:01:33.033
but also your father,
a playwright.

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:35.300
-Mm-hmm.
-Do you feel like you learned

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:37.968
the discipline of writing
from him?

00:01:37.968 --> 00:01:40.400
-I learned insomnia.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.501
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:45.234
-And staying up late writing,
no sleep.

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:48.434
But I don't know.
I think maybe a little bit.

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:49.901
But...
-So he's not --

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:51.534
'Cause every now and then,
we have a writer on the show,

00:01:51.534 --> 00:01:52.801
and they talk about
their schedule

00:01:52.801 --> 00:01:54.267
and how they wake up
first thing in the morning,

00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:55.567
and they start writing.
That's not you?

00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:56.901
-Mnh-mnh.
-Not your dad, either?

00:01:56.901 --> 00:01:58.200
-No.
-Okay.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.767
-Late at night.
-Late at night.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:02.067
So that probably made it hard
for him to criticize you

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.033
for late nights
and procrastinating

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:05.200
and leaving it
to the last minute.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.300
-No, he would prefer
that I would, like,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.601
watch a show with him
at midnight.

00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:12.567
I was 6 years old,
watching "48 Hours."

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:14.133
It was a good time.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.834
-"48 Hours,"
the television show?

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:17.367
-Uh-huh.
-Okay, great.

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.567
I was going to say "or the
movie," but both are bad.

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:20.667
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:21.934
-Both are not right
for a 6-year-old.

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:23.868
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.868 --> 00:02:27.434
-And I read somewhere
that your dad would pick --

00:02:27.434 --> 00:02:28.901
was it a word of the week?

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.901
-Yeah.
I mean, every week or so.

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:33.033
He still does it.
But my dad, you know,

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:38.300
he's a writer, and he loves
words, big words in particular.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.567
And so he would try to, like,
fit his new vocabulary word

00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:44.000
into every sentence -- like,
literally every sentence.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.434
So I would be, you know, doing
my homework, and he would go,

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:51.167
"You know,
you're sounding quite laconic."

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:52.601
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:55.801
And I would be like, "I just
want help dividing fractions.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:57.834
I don't know what this is."
And you have to remember,

00:02:57.834 --> 00:03:00.267
he is a Black man
from '60s Detroit,

00:03:00.267 --> 00:03:02.701
so he would speak, like, normal.

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:05.534
He would just add in these words
no one has ever heard of.

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:09.667
So he would be like, "You know,
that Negro was loquacious."

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:12.467
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.467 --> 00:03:13.467
I learned a lot of words
from him.

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:14.767
-Yeah, that's very good.

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:16.167
Well, obviously,
it's paying off.

00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:21.467
So this is inspired
by a real story you read about.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.701
I guess would be --
I mean, you're young now,

00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:25.701
so you were incredibly young
when you --

00:03:25.701 --> 00:03:27.067
Because this is about, what,
five, six years ago?

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:30.501
How long ago?
-Yeah, so a case broke in 2016

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:33.334
in the Bay Area
where a young girl of color

00:03:33.334 --> 00:03:37.067
was sexually exploited
by Bay Area police officers.

00:03:37.067 --> 00:03:38.801
And I was a young teenager
at the time.

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:41.067
I'm born and raised in Oakland.

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.501
So I paid a lot of attention
to this case,

00:03:43.501 --> 00:03:47.534
and I was struck by the way
that the media talked about it

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:50.701
and the lack of focus
on both this young girl

00:03:50.701 --> 00:03:53.434
and the harm done to her.
And also, you know,

00:03:53.434 --> 00:03:56.267
the thousands of other girls
and women who this happens to,

00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:57.934
and we never get to hear it.

00:03:57.934 --> 00:04:00.167
-And you wrote it from
the main character Kiara's

00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:01.601
first-person perspective.
-Yes.

00:04:01.601 --> 00:04:05.334
-Is that a decision that you
made early on in the process?

00:04:05.334 --> 00:04:07.734
-First decision.
I like to journal

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:10.734
from my characters' perspectives
and just, like,

00:04:10.734 --> 00:04:12.200
immerse myself entirely.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.133
So I spent three months kind of
being inside of them,

00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:17.234
and I think that's part of
the special thing about fiction

00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:20.000
is you just get to exist
in someone's head.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.334
-Do you journal before you start
writing the novel?

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:23.734
Do you spend all that time --
-And throughout.

00:04:23.734 --> 00:04:24.934
-Okay, gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:04:24.934 --> 00:04:26.834
-And do you find yourself
discovering new things

00:04:26.834 --> 00:04:28.100
about a character
of your own creation

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.167
throughout that process?
-Yeah, like, just journaling

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:32.734
about what they ate in the
morning and that kind of thing.

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:35.501
Like, it never ends up on
the page, but it's in my head.

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:38.334
-Well, it obviously pays off.
There's a lot of

00:04:38.334 --> 00:04:40.200
wonderful things being written
about this book already.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.167
I don't want to jinx it,
but it's very likely

00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:43.701
going to be on
the bestseller list.

00:04:43.701 --> 00:04:46.033
And we're just so honored to
have you here talking about it.

00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:47.267
Thank you so much
and congratulations.

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:49.167
-Thank you for having me!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.234
-You guys, that's Leila Mottley.

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:54.100
"Nightcrawling" is available now
wherever you buy your books.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.267
But please support local
and independent booksellers.

00:04:56.267 --> 00:04:57.968
We'll be back
with more "Late Night."

